    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build is green (`AcceptanceTest` is the only concrete test class; `AcceptanceBase` is abstract and `AuditLogCapture` is a helper).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `riskFlag` field on the owner read model so it returns `true` when any of the three conditions hold, `false` otherwise.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a `riskFlag` boolean `readOnly` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains `getRiskFlag()`/`setRiskFlag()` and it's serialized on `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n2. **`util/EmailNormalizer.java`** \u2014 Added `hasDisposableAdjacentDomain(email)`. The existing hard blocklist (`hasDisposableDomain`) causes a `400` at create, so a truly disposable address can never persist \u2014 meaning \"disposable-adjacent\" had to be the distinct, softer notion the spec calls for. It flags a domain that is *not itself* blocklisted but is closely related to one: a subdomain of a blocked domain (`x.mailinator.com`) or a sibling sharing a blocked provider's second-level label under a different TLD (`mailinator.net`).\n\n3. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added a `riskFlag(Owner)` default method wired via `@Mapping`. It ORs the three signals, reusing the already-computed `possibleDuplicate` flag, the `capacityWarning` flag (which is exactly \"city over its soft capacity\" \u2014 40+ owners), and the new disposable-adjacent email check. All three underlying flags are treated null-safely (legacy/seed owners \u2192 not set), and the result is always a non-null boolean.\n\nThe one existing test (`coreRiskFlagFalseNormally`) \u2014 a unique, email-less owner in an empty city \u2014 correctly yields `riskFlag = false`.",
